"use strict";(self.webpackChunkspa_template=self.webpackChunkspa_template||[]).push([[985],{7985:s=>{s.exports=JSON.parse('{"created":"2018-07-06T14:38:00","modified":"2018-07-06T14:38:00","id":1039,"title":"Working with Nuget Package Source - Local and Remote","article":"<p>If using more than one nuget feed, so more than just <a href=\\"https://www.nuget.org/api/v2/\\">https://www.nuget.org/api/v2/</a> there are a few ways to add it:</p>\\n<ul>\\n<li>Add a <code>nuget.config</code> file to the solution/project root</li>\\n<li>Edit the users personal nuget config at <code>%appdata%\\\\NuGet\\\\NuGet.Config</code> or <code>~/.nuget/NuGet/NuGet.Config</code></li>\\n<li>Edit the machine nuget config at <code>%ProgramFiles(x86)%\\\\NuGet\\\\Config</code> or <code>$XDG_DATA_HOME</code></li>\\n</ul>\\n<h2 id=\\"the-problem\\">The Problem</h2>\\n<p>Working on multiple machines/environments and as WSL becomes more popular the inevitable switch between Windows and Linux, we hit a few issues.</p>\\n<p>The normal one is the package sources you use on your developer machine might be different to what you use on a build server (production server is less important as the code is built prior to shipping). Say on your dev machine you have a local packages folder that you use for testing but on the build server you have MyGet or a similar service serving your prouction packages.</p>\\n<p>Another is that you might be building in linux or Windows, this is more common now WSL is available, where the the paths of the nuget sources would be different for the different OS.</p>\\n<p>Finally &#39;your&#39; local setup might be different to &#39;my&#39; local setup, meaning the local references wont work out of the box.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"solutionproject-level\\">Solution/Project Level</h2>\\n<p>In theory the best option is to keep it near the solution so that it is checked in/out with source control and the app has everything it needs to build, we can then add multiple package sources that work for all environments.</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-xml\\"><span class=\\"hljs-meta\\">&lt;?xml version=\\"1.0\\" encoding=\\"utf-8\\"?&gt;</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">configuration</span>&gt;</span>\\n  <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">packageSources</span>&gt;</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">add</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">key</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"nuget.org\\"</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">value</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"https://www.nuget.org/api/v2/\\"</span> /&gt;</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">add</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">key</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"remote\\"</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">value</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"https://www.myget.com/blah\\"</span> /&gt;</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">add</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">key</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"local-win\\"</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">value</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"D:\\\\Dev\\\\Code\\\\My\\\\Nuget\\"</span> /&gt;</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">add</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">key</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"local-linux\\"</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">value</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"/mnt/d/Dev/Code/My/Nuget\\"</span> /&gt;</span>\\n  <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;/<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">packageSources</span>&gt;</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;/<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">configuration</span>&gt;</span></code></pre>\\n<p>There are two &#39;potential&#39; caveats with this, to ensure you are getting the local package that you have been editing you have used a higher version number for that package, so it misses the remote source and then skips to the next source in the list. This is normal expectation though, so wouldnt be too difficult to adhere too.</p>\\n<p>The other issue is as above, &#39;your&#39; local machine and &#39;my&#39; local machine might have their local nuget feeds in different places. </p>\\n<h2 id=\\"user-level\\">User level</h2>\\n<p>Another option is to place these settings in the user nuget config, the caveats there being the version numbers but also it means having to set a server/pc each time with this step, again not impossible to overcome but another step.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"hybrid---best-solution\\">Hybrid - &quot;Best&quot; Solution</h2>\\n<p>Arguably the best solution I could see was mixing the two as the config settings cascade down. In the solution/project <code>./nuget.config</code> file place the remote sources, but in the user <code>~/.nuget/NuGet/NuGet.Config</code> (or <code>%appdata%\\\\NuGet\\\\NuGet.Config</code>) place the local sources.</p>\\n<p>The benefits mean that new servers/pc&#39;s are built with little additional effort and require just the code, but if requiring local edits of nuget packages the sources are still available.</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-xml\\"><span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">&lt;!-- linux user nuget.config --&gt;</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-meta\\">&lt;?xml version=\\"1.0\\" encoding=\\"utf-8\\"?&gt;</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">configuration</span>&gt;</span>\\n  <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">packageSources</span>&gt;</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">add</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">key</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"nuget.org\\"</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">value</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"https://www.nuget.org/api/v2/\\"</span> /&gt;</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">add</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">key</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"local\\"</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">value</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"/mnt/d/Dev/Code/My/Nuget\\"</span> /&gt;</span>\\n  <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;/<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">packageSources</span>&gt;</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;/<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">configuration</span>&gt;</span>\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">&lt;!-- windows user nuget.config --&gt;</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-meta\\">&lt;?xml version=\\"1.0\\" encoding=\\"utf-8\\"?&gt;</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">configuration</span>&gt;</span>\\n  <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">packageSources</span>&gt;</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">add</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">key</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"nuget.org\\"</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">value</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"https://www.nuget.org/api/v2/\\"</span> /&gt;</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">add</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">key</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"local\\"</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">value</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"D:\\\\Dev\\\\Code\\\\My\\\\Nuget\\"</span> /&gt;</span>\\n  <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;/<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">packageSources</span>&gt;</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;/<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">configuration</span>&gt;</span>\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">&lt;!-- solution/project nuget.config --&gt;</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-meta\\">&lt;?xml version=\\"1.0\\" encoding=\\"utf-8\\"?&gt;</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">configuration</span>&gt;</span>\\n  <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">packageSources</span>&gt;</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">add</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">key</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"nuget.org\\"</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">value</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"https://www.nuget.org/api/v2/\\"</span> /&gt;</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">add</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">key</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"remote\\"</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">value</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"https://www.myget.com/blah\\"</span> /&gt;</span>\\n  <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;/<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">packageSources</span>&gt;</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;/<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">configuration</span>&gt;</span></code></pre>\\n<p>As the developers that work with local nuget packages we tend to store them in a single location, at least from my experience, so the above user config files will work for all those packages aka less work! You can however add as many local sources as required.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"summary\\">Summary</h2>\\n<p>The best solution is probably the hybrid as it has the least downsides. There are other options such as using the nuget cli to add the references, however the nuget cli currently requires a mono build on Linux/Mac and has limited functionality, so going the file route makes sense.</p>\\n","showInResults":true,"tags":[]}')}}]);